\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Cycle decimation and periodic covariance}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Take the unit-weight cycle on #{\mathbb Z/(2N)}, with
#{N\ge3} and precision #{Q=L_{C_{2N}}+a\bbI}, where
#{a=m^2>0}. The neighbor coefficient is #{1} and the diagonal
coefficient is #{2+a}, so the shape is #{t=(2+a)/2}. Thus
#{Q=P_t}, the \vocabk{unit-coupling cycle operator}{fdip-00AW}
on this cycle, and the decimation factor is #{2}. The group
#{G=C_N} acts by two-site rotations. It preserves the even
retained vertices and the odd eliminated vertices. Write
#{x_j=\phi_{2j}}, #{y_j=\phi_{2j+1}}; indices #{j} lie in
#{\mathbb Z/N}. For the shift #{(Tz)_j=z_{j-1}} on the
retained cycle, the blocks are
##{A=C=(2+a)\bbI,\qquad B=-(\bbI+T),\qquad B^{\dagger}=-(\bbI+T^{-1}).}
Thus the [equivariant Schur formula](fdip-0056) gives
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=(2+a)\bbI-(2+a)^{-1}(2\bbI+T+T^{-1})\\
 &=\frac{1}{2+a}\,P^K_{T_2(t)},
\end{aligned}}
where #{T_2(t)=2t^2-1} and
#{P^K_s=2s\,\bbI-(T+T^{-1})} is the same operator on the retained
sites. Since #{2t=2+a}, the diagonal
#{2T_2(t)/(2+a)} equals #{(2+a)-2/(2+a)}, so the two
displays agree. The retained shape is #{T_2(t)} and the
retained coupling is #{1/(2+a)}. The retained mass, the
diagonal minus twice that coupling, equals
##{\frac{a(a+4)}{a+2}=(2+a)-\frac{4}{2+a}>0.}
The restriction #{Q_{RR}=(2+a)\bbI} has no off-diagonal entries,
while #{S} has entries #{-1/(2+a)} between adjacent retained
vertices. The retained set is stable under #{G}, but the
marginal precision is induced by elimination. This is the
#{q=2} case of \ref{fdip-00B3}, with neighbor value #{1} and
diagonal #{2+a}.}

\tikzfigs{0.75}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  retained/.style={circle,draw,fill=black!18,minimum size=6mm,inner sep=1pt},
  removed/.style={circle,draw,dashed,minimum size=6mm,inner sep=1pt},
  every node/.style={font=\small}
]
\node[retained] (v0) at (0,1.25) {$0$};
\node[removed] (v1) at (1.08,0.625) {$1$};
\node[retained] (v2) at (1.08,-0.625) {$2$};
\node[removed] (v3) at (0,-1.25) {$3$};
\node[retained] (v4) at (-1.08,-0.625) {$4$};
\node[removed] (v5) at (-1.08,0.625) {$5$};
\draw (v0)--(v1)--(v2)--(v3)--(v4)--(v5)--(v0);
\draw[-{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (1.6,0)--node[above,align=center]
 {eliminate\\odd vertices} (3.4,0);
\node[retained] (r0) at (4.9,1.25) {$0$};
\node[retained] (r2) at (5.98,-0.625) {$2$};
\node[retained] (r4) at (3.82,-0.625) {$4$};
\draw (r0)--(r2)--(r4)--(r0);
\node at (0,-1.9) {$w=1,\quad m^2=a$};
\node at (4.9,-1.9) {$\frac{1}{2+a},\ \frac{a(a+4)}{a+2}$};
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The six-cycle illustrates #{N=3}. Shaded even vertices remain;
dashed odd vertices are integrated out. Each triangle edge has
coupling #{1/(2+a)}, and the mass squared changes from #{a} to
#{a(a+4)/(a+2)}.}

\p{The finite Gaussian normalization is retained exactly:
#{Z_Q=Z_CZ_S} with
#{Z_C=\bigl(\frac{2\pi}{2+a}\bigr)^{N/2}}, and the retained
law is #{\mu_S}. The retained coupling and the retained mass
are both part of this law. Dividing #{S} by the coupling
#{1/(2+a)} compares the shape, but changes the field
covariance by a scalar unless the corresponding field
rescaling is also made.}

\p{Put #{2+a=2\cosh\omega} with #{\omega>0}. The shape is then
#{t=\cosh\omega}, and the retained shape is
#{T_2(t)=\cosh(2\omega)}. Twice that shape equals
##{(2+a)^2-2=2\cosh(2\omega).}
The doubled parameter has an exact finite-ring interpretation.
For #{M\ge3}, the covariance of precision
#{w(2\cosh\eta\,\bbI-T-T^{-1})} on #{\mathbb Z/M}, with
#{w,\eta>0}, is the periodic kernel
##{\begin{aligned}
K_{M,w,\eta}(r)
 &=\frac{\ue^{-\eta r}+\ue^{-\eta(M-r)}}
 {2w\sinh\eta\,(1-\ue^{-M\eta})},\\
&\hspace{24mm}0\le r\le M-1.
\end{aligned}}
For the original and retained fields this gives
##{(Q^{-1})_{0,2r}
 =K_{2N,1,\omega}(2r)
 =K_{N,\,1/(2+a),\,2\omega}(r)
 =(S^{-1})_{0,r}.}
The equality is between covariances with their actual
couplings. Here #{1/(2+a)=1/(2\cosh\omega)}.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{D=2w\sinh\eta\,(1-\ue^{-M\eta})} and, for an integer
#{r},
#{g(r)=\ue^{-\eta r}+\ue^{-\eta(M-r)}}. On a representative
#{0\le r\le M-1} the kernel is #{K(r)=g(r)/D}, and the value
at the cycle site #{0} is also the value #{g(M)}. For every
integer #{r},
##{g(r+1)+g(r-1)=2\cosh\eta\,g(r),}
because each exponential is multiplied by #{\ue^{\pm\eta}} under
a unit shift and those factors sum to #{2\cosh\eta}. Thus
#{2\cosh\eta\,g(r)-g(r-1)-g(r+1)=0}. For
#{r=1,\ldots,M-1} the two cycle neighbors are represented by
#{r-1} and #{r+1}, using #{g(M)=g(0)} at the last site, so
##{w\bigl(2\cosh\eta\,K(r)-K(r-1)-K(r+1)\bigr)=0.}
At #{r=0} the previous site is #{M-1}. Its value is
#{g(M-1)}, and #{g(1)=g(M-1)}, so the cycle combination is
#{2\cosh\eta\,g(0)-2g(1)}. The identities
#{\cosh\eta-\ue^{-\eta}=\sinh\eta} and
#{\cosh\eta-\ue^{\eta}=-\sinh\eta} give
##{\cosh\eta\,g(0)-g(1)=\sinh\eta\,(1-\ue^{-M\eta}).}
Multiplying by #{2w/D} yields #{1}. The precision is positive
for #{w,\eta>0}, so this unit source shows that #{K} is its
inverse on #{\mathbb Z/M}.}

\p{For #{M=2N} and even separation #{2r}, the numerator
#{\ue^{-\omega(2r)}+\ue^{-\omega(2N-2r)}} is the numerator of
#{K_{N,\,1/(2\cosh\omega),\,2\omega}(r)}. The denominators
agree because
##{2\cdot\frac{1}{2\cosh\omega}\cdot\sinh(2\omega)
 =2\sinh\omega.}
Thus #{K_{2N,1,\omega}(2r)} equals the retained kernel. Both
are inverses of the stated precisions, so the covariance
identity holds.}
}

\p{Remark. The same kernel is the sum of
#{h(n)=\ue^{-\eta|n|}/(2w\sinh\eta)} over the periods
#{n=r+kM}. That sum is not used above.}

\p{In the convention that correlation length is the reciprocal
decay rate of these two-exponential kernels, it changes from
#{1/\omega} fine lattice units to #{1/(2\omega)} coarse
lattice units. The finite ring has two winding directions, so
its covariance is not a single exponential in separation. If
the old spacing is #{\ell}, the new spacing is #{2\ell}; the
physical decay length #{\ell / \omega=(2\ell)/(2\omega)} is
unchanged.}

\p{The bound #{N\ge3} keeps the reduced graph a simple cycle.
At #{N=2}, #{T=T^{-1}} and two eliminated vertices connect the
same retained pair, producing two parallel edges of coupling
#{1/(2+a)}, or one edge of coupling #{2/(2+a)}. At #{a=0},
the algebraic formula still gives retained coupling #{1/2}
and retained mass #{0}, the two-resistors-in-series limit.
The full precision then has a constant zero mode and no
normalized full-space Gaussian law. Pinning or a mean-zero
constraint must be specified as in
[constrained massless laws](fdip-003V); the positive-mass covariance
formula is not a finite limit on the full field space.}

\p{This is a direct cycle calculation within
\newvocab{Kron reduction}, the Schur reduction of graph Laplacians
with possible positive diagonal terms.
Closure follows from \citet{Lemma 2.1, pp. 5-6}{dorfler2011kron}.
Successive and simultaneous eliminations agree by
\citet{Lemma 3.3, p. 14}{dorfler2011kron}.
In that source the diagonal mass corresponds to a
shunt conductance or graph self-loop. In the incidence convention
used here an edge with identical endpoints has zero difference;
the mass is therefore kept as the separate term #{a\bbI}.
The source supplies the general reduction context, while the
periodic covariance and scale statements are proved above.}
